1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,200
No one wants a handmade engine block or jet turbine.

2
00:00:06,220 --> 00:00:08,840
We want precision things made by machines.

3
00:00:09,720 --> 00:00:11,840
Machine tools built our modern world.

4
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:14,660
Our lifestyle wouldn't exist without them.

5
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:18,260
Now, machine tools on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Simply put, machine tools are power-driven machines of all shapes and sizes,

7
00:00:47,880 --> 00:00:49,800
primarily used to create metal parts.

8
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:55,720
Those metal parts might be the steel mold for a plastic automobile headlight.

9
00:00:56,720 --> 00:00:59,980
They might be the aluminum supports for a jet airplane body.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,920
or they might be parts to another machine tool.

11
00:01:06,860 --> 00:01:10,600
Machine tools have left their legacy cut, stamped, and shaped

12
00:01:10,601 --> 00:01:13,000
into the metal of every imaginable product.

13
00:01:14,060 --> 00:01:20,520
It produces thousands of parts that go into the makeup of various items that we utilize every day.

14
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:24,340
If there's no machine tool, everything would have to be shaped by hand,

15
00:01:24,341 --> 00:01:30,280
hand scraped, fitted, or filed into specific shape to fit into mating parts.

16
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:36,120
All these inventions that came about in the late 19th, early 20th century

17
00:01:36,121 --> 00:01:41,280
might not have come about as easily as they had if it were not for machine tools.

18
00:01:42,900 --> 00:01:45,680
Machine tools are at their core tools.

19
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:49,060
If you think about a tool like a hand drill,

20
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:52,960
a machine tool takes that drill's process and mechanizes it

21
00:01:52,961 --> 00:01:57,740
and performs it more precisely and more efficiently than a human ever could.

22
00:01:59,240 --> 00:02:03,140
The most widely used machine tools today are called machining centers.

23
00:02:04,300 --> 00:02:08,300
They perform a host of operations from drilling to milling to boring.

24
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:13,240
A raw block of metal goes in, a finished part comes out.

25
00:02:16,380 --> 00:02:20,220
One place where this began was the Cincinnati Screw and Tap Company,

26
00:02:20,221 --> 00:02:23,680
founded in 1884 by George Mueller and Fred Holtz.

27
00:02:25,180 --> 00:02:30,220
Today, the company they started is one of the world's leading machine tool manufacturers.

28
00:02:31,320 --> 00:02:35,580
What we do here in Cincinnati is we design, manufacture, and assemble

29
00:02:35,581 --> 00:02:38,320
all of our machine tools built in the United States.

30
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:43,700
This particular machine is called the U5 because it can operate on five sites of a part.

31
00:02:44,180 --> 00:02:47,140
And these days, our customers, in trying to reduce setup,

32
00:02:47,141 --> 00:02:49,980
they want to complete a part in one operation.

33
00:02:50,220 --> 00:02:53,220
Hence, they want to be able to operate on all five sides of the part.

34
00:02:54,320 --> 00:02:58,140
Typical customers of this kind of machine tool are tool and die manufacturers.

35
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:05,060
The machine would come in and would actually shape the die, for example, in the shape of a doorframe for a car.

36
00:03:06,660 --> 00:03:12,740
There are two Pentium microprocessors running the computer that runs this computer numerical control machine.

37
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:15,940
The computer directs all the operations of the machine tool.

38
00:03:15,941 --> 00:03:22,960
There might be up to 50,000 unique tool points all blended together in one motion.

39
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:27,520
You can store literally hundreds of thousands of data points in a machine like this.

40
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:31,360
Machine tools don't come cheap.

41
00:03:31,900 --> 00:03:36,800
This one starts at close to a million dollars, and that price can quickly double as features are added.

42
00:03:41,940 --> 00:03:45,680
Also building the machine tool business was R.K. LeBlond.

43
00:03:45,940 --> 00:03:50,180
which opened in Cincinnati in 1887 with just a handful of employees.

44
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:57,720
Today, it's the Makino Company, one of the largest machine tool manufacturers in the world.

45
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:03,760
Among other things, its tools make precision parts for the space shuttle program.

46
00:04:04,960 --> 00:04:08,660
This is one of the stages of an impeller for the main shuttle engine.

47
00:04:09,180 --> 00:04:11,340
What happens is we take three of these, stack them together,

48
00:04:11,341 --> 00:04:17,180
and together they'll generate about 76,000 horsepower running at 36,000 RPM.

49
00:04:17,820 --> 00:04:21,120
This is a turbo pump for the oxygen on the main shuttle engine

50
00:04:21,121 --> 00:04:23,260
and it has the ability to drain a swimming pool,

51
00:04:23,860 --> 00:04:26,320
an Olympic-sized swimming pool, in 59 seconds.

52
00:04:28,200 --> 00:04:31,320
The part is machined from a solid piece of titanium

53
00:04:31,321 --> 00:04:33,920
that can cost as much as $50,000.

54
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:40,160
The machining center cuts it to precise, extremely complex specifications.

55
00:04:41,340 --> 00:04:46,700
It takes about 13 or 14 days straight just to mill this part.

56
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:48,600
So this is probably as difficult as it gets.

57
00:04:51,640 --> 00:04:55,380
Machine tools are really extensions of man's most basic tools,

58
00:04:55,760 --> 00:04:57,560
the human hand and the human mind.

59
00:04:59,460 --> 00:05:03,040
Primitive forms of machine tools have been around for thousands of years.

60
00:05:04,740 --> 00:05:09,660
Crude bracelets were turned as early as 100 B.C. by the Laten Kelts.

61
00:05:11,340 --> 00:05:15,020
There are drawings of sword grinders dating back to the Dark Ages.

62
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:24,420
In the 1600s, sawmills and gristmills, early forms of machine tools, were built next to streams and powered by falling water.

63
00:05:26,020 --> 00:05:31,900
In the 1680s, Peter the Great collected simple machine tools that could decorate watches and trinkets.

64
00:05:33,680 --> 00:05:41,280
But the story of modern machine tools, those strong enough to shape heavy metal, begins with the Industrial Revolution in England.

65
00:05:41,340 --> 00:05:51,060
in the 1700s. These tools depended upon a new source of power, steam. In 1765, James

66
00:05:51,061 --> 00:05:55,651
Watt created a steam engine, dependable enough to power a machine.

67
00:05:55,652 --> 00:05:57,320
His engine harnessed the

68
00:05:57,321 --> 00:06:02,500
up and down action of the piston and used it to turn a crankshaft, a process called

69
00:06:02,501 --> 00:06:03,473
rotary power.

70
00:06:03,474 --> 00:06:09,460
Watt's rotating crankshaft would be what powered the first modern machine tools.

71
00:06:11,340 --> 00:06:18,080
The real beauty of the steam engines, at least related to the machine industry, is that you could put a steam engine anywhere.

72
00:06:18,600 --> 00:06:22,900
You didn't need a water source. You didn't need a mill run to operate a wheel.

73
00:06:23,180 --> 00:06:27,080
You could put a steam engine of any size in your factory and do machining.

74
00:06:28,720 --> 00:06:36,220
It was really an innovation to have a steam engine that could be hooked up almost anywhere.

75
00:06:37,060 --> 00:06:41,180
Steam engines, however, were difficult to run.

76
00:06:41,360 --> 00:06:43,160
They took a long time to get started.

77
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:45,380
They had to be well lubricated.

78
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:46,700
They were loud.

79
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:49,920
Unfortunately, they were quite dirty.

80
00:06:51,060 --> 00:06:52,860
They were surely not safe.

81
00:06:55,060 --> 00:06:58,920
In spite of their shortcomings, within 50 years of Watts' steam engine,

82
00:06:59,420 --> 00:07:02,140
all the basic modern machine tools would be invented,

83
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:04,240
most of them powered by steam.

84
00:07:05,240 --> 00:07:08,160
Some would be adaptations of earlier woodworking tools.

85
00:07:08,900 --> 00:07:10,980
Some would be completely new ideas.

86
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:19,820
The mid-1700s through the early 1900s would be the single greatest period of machine tool invention and design.

87
00:07:23,020 --> 00:07:28,980
We think that we are so far advanced today, and we are so clever and so smart,

88
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:32,360
and have so much technology behind us,

89
00:07:32,361 --> 00:07:44,260
And yet the early people that devised the first machine tools really were very, very bright individuals.

90
00:07:44,740 --> 00:07:51,460
It was a, by guessing, by golly type of thing that eventually produced something of value.

91
00:07:52,480 --> 00:08:01,080
They didn't have the college degrees that are evident today, but by golly they had a lot of ingenuity and they used it.

92
00:08:02,360 --> 00:08:11,840
Machine tools changed the world by making possible the manufacture of all kinds of goods,

93
00:08:11,841 --> 00:08:16,300
which prior to the Industrial Revolution had only been within the realm of the wealthy.

94
00:08:17,240 --> 00:08:21,200
Suddenly, they could be produced in vast quantities at low cost.

95
00:08:23,540 --> 00:08:28,300
The machine tools that made this possible can be broken down into seven basic types.

96
00:08:28,301 --> 00:08:33,940
The earliest are the turning machines, also known as lathes and boards.

97
00:08:35,020 --> 00:08:40,180
The second were the planers and shapers, built to work with metal much like a carpenter's plan.

98
00:08:40,780 --> 00:08:44,520
Third was the most durable of all machine tools, the milling machine.

99
00:08:45,360 --> 00:08:48,660
Like the planer and shaper, it smooths and shapes metal parts.

100
00:08:48,661 --> 00:08:52,100
Four other tools round out the seven.

101
00:08:52,880 --> 00:09:00,040
Drilling machines, grinders, power saws, and large metal shaping tools known as punch presses.

102
00:09:00,840 --> 00:09:04,960
All were invented by men working with little more than intuition and a deadline.

103
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:08,560
When we come back, we'll take a look at them all.

104
00:09:11,680 --> 00:09:16,140
The earliest object ever found made by a turning machine was a bowl.

105
00:09:16,141 --> 00:09:19,540
Discovered in Italy, made in 700 B.C.

106
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:23,380
Machine tools will return on Modern Marvels.

107
00:09:24,620 --> 00:09:29,760
It is difficult to trace the development of a machine tool in a single clear line.

108
00:09:31,700 --> 00:09:34,960
People invented machine tools for their own private use.

109
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:38,820
To make some specific product, never intending to sell them.

110
00:09:40,100 --> 00:09:42,680
Also, early machinists were concentrated together.

111
00:09:42,681 --> 00:09:46,560
As they visited one another's shops, they saw and borrowed ideas.

112
00:09:47,200 --> 00:09:51,840
There was little respect for patents, and laws that protected machine tools were hard to enforce.

113
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:57,500
Still, it is clear that the first modern machine tool to be invented was the turning machine,

114
00:09:58,040 --> 00:09:59,620
commonly called a boring machine.

115
00:10:03,420 --> 00:10:06,220
Its invention is credited to John Wilkinson,

116
00:10:06,820 --> 00:10:10,140
known in the 1760s as the great Stafford Iron Master,

117
00:10:10,380 --> 00:10:12,480
because of his skill casting iron.

118
00:10:12,680 --> 00:10:19,500
Wilkinson had been pouring iron castings since his 20s when he built his first blast furnace.

119
00:10:21,120 --> 00:10:27,620
In 1775, realizing it would help to make cannons if he could bore the inside of the rough barrels smooth,

120
00:10:28,420 --> 00:10:32,000
he used Watt's steam engine to construct the first boring machine.

121
00:10:34,180 --> 00:10:36,340
Other boring machines soon followed.

122
00:10:37,260 --> 00:10:38,600
Most were used to make cannons.

123
00:10:39,960 --> 00:10:42,400
Weapons were often the first use for machine tools.

124
00:10:42,680 --> 00:10:51,380
However, Wilkinson's machine was also used to improve the steam engine by boring larger pistons.

125
00:10:51,381 --> 00:10:55,580
Like many machine tools, it improved the machine that had helped give it life.

126
00:10:58,080 --> 00:11:02,060
Wilkinson's boring machine was soon followed by a metal cutting lathe.

127
00:11:03,100 --> 00:11:11,180
The lathe is probably the most versatile machine and the most widely used of all the machine tools

128
00:11:11,181 --> 00:11:14,680
because so many things are cylindrical in nature,

129
00:11:15,120 --> 00:11:22,000
and its primary purpose is to cut metal down to a specific diameter.

130
00:11:22,820 --> 00:11:26,920
This is important if you're going to make an axle shaft, for instance,

131
00:11:27,560 --> 00:11:30,400
for a wagon so that the wagon wheel would fit on.

132
00:11:31,640 --> 00:11:36,600
It's important in the manufacture of any type of automobile parts,

133
00:11:37,020 --> 00:11:38,980
transmissions, engines.

134
00:11:38,981 --> 00:11:44,740
virtually everything is basically cylindrical in nature,

135
00:11:45,320 --> 00:11:48,300
and this is turned on a lathe.

136
00:11:51,780 --> 00:11:54,300
Wilkinson, however, did not invent the lathe.

137
00:11:54,600 --> 00:11:56,060
That fell to another Englishman.

138
00:11:57,840 --> 00:12:02,700
Henry Maudsley had apprenticed to the famous lockmaker Joseph Brahma in 1791.

139
00:12:04,460 --> 00:12:06,180
Maudsley soon became Brahma's foreman,

140
00:12:06,380 --> 00:12:08,660
but when Maudsley was refused a raise,

141
00:12:08,980 --> 00:12:10,360
He struck out on his own.

142
00:12:11,440 --> 00:12:16,980
In 1797, inspired by the early wooden lathes, Maudsley created the first metal lathe.

143
00:12:17,300 --> 00:12:18,740
It was used to cut screws.

144
00:12:20,780 --> 00:12:28,400
In the early 1800s, an American named Tom Blanchard created a new kind of lathe to machine heavy wood gun stocks.

145
00:12:28,920 --> 00:12:30,620
It was called a pattern lathe.

146
00:12:31,620 --> 00:12:35,580
He would put in essentially a pattern piece in the top of the machine,

147
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:42,940
and the lathe would follow this pattern to create another identical gunstock in 20, 25 minutes.

148
00:12:43,980 --> 00:12:47,740
It was this type of machining that they were going for, especially in the firearms industry,

149
00:12:47,900 --> 00:12:50,400
where you could pump out these pieces and then assemble them.

150
00:12:51,840 --> 00:12:54,960
Lathes became the focus of early productivity experts.

151
00:12:55,720 --> 00:12:59,500
Their handles were moved to one side, so machinists wouldn't have to walk around them.

152
00:13:00,620 --> 00:13:06,540
In 1845, Stephen Fitch of Middlefield, Connecticut built the first turret lathe,

153
00:13:06,541 --> 00:13:10,460
in which several tools could be held and rotated as the cutting progressed.

154
00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:17,580
In 1887, the R.K. LeBlond Company opened in Cincinnati, Ohio,

155
00:13:18,060 --> 00:13:20,860
a city that would become famous for machine tool manufacturing.

156
00:13:23,840 --> 00:13:27,460
LeBlond would become the largest manufacturer of lathes in the world.

157
00:13:27,461 --> 00:13:34,120
This is a 1903 R.K. LeBlanc lathe. It was manufactured here in Cincinnati.

158
00:13:35,460 --> 00:13:38,380
The lathe is the oldest form of all machine tools.

159
00:13:38,860 --> 00:13:45,340
The idea of actually turning an object to shape it into a round shape or a smoother round shape

160
00:13:45,341 --> 00:13:48,180
goes back millennia, even to the time of the first wheels.

161
00:13:51,320 --> 00:13:57,320
Two critical aspects of all lathes are their speed, the rate at which the stock turns, and their feed.

162
00:13:57,460 --> 00:13:59,580
how fast the tool is moved as it cuts.

163
00:14:00,960 --> 00:14:04,520
The speed of the lathe is controlled by the pulley size,

164
00:14:04,620 --> 00:14:06,820
which is set on the lathe itself.

165
00:14:07,300 --> 00:14:10,360
The smaller the pulley wheel that it's set on the lathe,

166
00:14:10,580 --> 00:14:12,320
the faster they are higher the RPMs.

167
00:14:12,520 --> 00:14:13,880
The larger, the slower.

168
00:14:14,580 --> 00:14:18,120
There's another pulley wheel system which controls the feed rate,

169
00:14:18,121 --> 00:14:21,400
the rate that the tool moves either one direction or another

170
00:14:21,401 --> 00:14:22,780
on the stock that's being worked.

171
00:14:22,781 --> 00:14:31,960
While their power has evolved from steam to diesel, and now electric, lathes still look

172
00:14:31,961 --> 00:14:36,500
much the same as they did 100 years ago.

173
00:14:36,501 --> 00:14:41,560
You can make a machine tool faster, you can make them more accurate, and you can make them

174
00:14:41,561 --> 00:14:48,220
more precise, but the principles behind cutting, shaping, and finishing a piece of metal have

175
00:14:48,221 --> 00:14:50,220
remained the same.

176
00:14:50,221 --> 00:14:58,360
The principles may have remained the same, but lathes did grow in size, the spindles on some reaching over 40 feet.

177
00:15:00,680 --> 00:15:06,080
And as with all machine tools, they were at their busiest during both world wars.

178
00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:14,780
As far as the Arcade LeBlanc company is concerned, they had a tremendous role in World War I and World War II.

179
00:15:14,781 --> 00:15:22,640
They produced what we call a gun boring lathe for the various artillery pieces that were used throughout the war.

180
00:15:23,160 --> 00:15:25,080
The big howitzers.

181
00:15:29,800 --> 00:15:34,480
Many believe World War II could not have been won without American machine tools.

182
00:15:34,481 --> 00:15:44,420
without the quality of machines that we had which produced quality parts we never could have

183
00:15:44,421 --> 00:15:53,260
provided the war material for england france and the australians the canadians they all used

184
00:15:53,261 --> 00:15:59,080
american equipment i think they made the difference between our winning the war or

185
00:15:59,081 --> 00:16:00,968
possibly losing the war.

186
00:16:00,969 --> 00:16:06,080
If we had not been able to supply virtually the rest of the world

187
00:16:06,081 --> 00:16:12,080
with the necessary war material, we might be saluting the Nazi flag today.

188
00:16:14,840 --> 00:16:20,040
Computers were added to lathes in the 1960s as they developed into the modern machining

189
00:16:20,041 --> 00:16:25,752
centers of today. Most modern turning machines are computer controlled.

190
00:16:25,753 --> 00:16:27,280
You have originally

191
00:16:27,281 --> 00:16:31,260
spindle speeds of 3,000 to 4,000 RPM. Now you're talking

192
00:16:31,261 --> 00:16:33,520
20,000 to 30,000 RPM.

193
00:16:34,900 --> 00:16:39,140
Almost all the things that rely on motion from electric motors

194
00:16:39,141 --> 00:16:42,740
involve parts that spin, and most of those parts are produced on lathes

195
00:16:42,741 --> 00:16:47,380
and turning machines. Lathes and boring machines

196
00:16:47,381 --> 00:16:51,040
did not develop in a vacuum. Other very different machine tools

197
00:16:51,041 --> 00:16:55,200
were developed at the same time. The second category, planers

198
00:16:55,201 --> 00:17:00,580
and shapers at one time were some of the most important machine tools.

199
00:17:00,581 --> 00:17:02,040
Planers and shapers

200
00:17:02,041 --> 00:17:08,800
can be compared to the carpenter's plane, used to smooth and cut flat metal surfaces.

201
00:17:08,801 --> 00:17:14,100
With planers, the work moves under the tool.

202
00:17:14,101 --> 00:17:19,280
With shapers, the tool moves over the work.

203
00:17:19,281 --> 00:17:24,720
James Fox, a one-time country butler turned machinist, is credited with building one of

204
00:17:24,721 --> 00:17:26,880
the first metal planers in 1814.

205
00:17:30,300 --> 00:17:36,500
One of the oldest planers is at the American Precision Museum in Windsor, Vermont, home

206
00:17:36,501 --> 00:17:39,680
to one of the world's largest collection of machine tools.

207
00:17:41,180 --> 00:17:42,840
Ed Battison is its founder.

208
00:17:45,200 --> 00:17:47,960
Most of the early machine tools have no maker's name on them.

209
00:17:48,420 --> 00:17:53,020
They were made by the people that needed them rather than the people that were making a

210
00:17:53,021 --> 00:17:53,940
market out of selling.

211
00:17:54,720 --> 00:17:59,780
We have an iron planer. It's operated entirely by hand, and it was made with file.

212
00:18:00,580 --> 00:18:03,160
It was cast a rough shape and then fired up.

213
00:18:03,580 --> 00:18:05,880
It would make all the shapes you can't make on a lathe.

214
00:18:09,880 --> 00:18:13,380
James Naismith was an Englishman who worked for Henry Maudsley,

215
00:18:14,020 --> 00:18:17,000
the same Henry Maudsley who invented the first lathe.

216
00:18:18,460 --> 00:18:24,480
After Maudsley's death in 1831, Naismith moved to Edinburgh and opened his own shop.

217
00:18:24,720 --> 00:18:31,720
He specialized in making paddle wheels for steamboats, parts too large to move under a planer.

218
00:18:31,721 --> 00:18:35,720
So in 1839, he invented a tool that moved over the part.

219
00:18:35,721 --> 00:18:37,720
He called it a steam hammer.

220
00:18:37,721 --> 00:18:41,720
In fact, it was the first metal shaper.

221
00:18:41,721 --> 00:18:46,720
This is a shaper from the American Tool Works, built in 1906 right here in Cincinnati.

222
00:18:46,721 --> 00:18:51,720
The shaper does a real basic type of job for people.

223
00:18:51,721 --> 00:18:55,120
It basically shapes a piece of stock.

224
00:18:55,980 --> 00:19:01,060
It goes through an enormous amount of movement just to perform this simple task, though.

225
00:19:01,420 --> 00:19:03,600
Shapers can eliminate simple filing.

226
00:19:04,200 --> 00:19:07,340
It can also make keyways or notches for fitted pieces.

227
00:19:08,400 --> 00:19:14,260
As the stock is moved left or right, the cutting tool is moved back and forth.

228
00:19:14,620 --> 00:19:19,600
When the cutting tool moves to the home position, to the rear, the table moves over gradually,

229
00:19:19,601 --> 00:19:24,520
and then the cutting tool moves forward again and takes another bite off of the stock itself.

230
00:19:26,700 --> 00:19:32,900
Shapers and planers played a critical role, not just in creating new products, but in creating new machine tools.

231
00:19:33,880 --> 00:19:39,040
One of those tools was the milling machine, the third category of machine tools.

232
00:19:40,120 --> 00:19:44,220
Unlike previous machine tools, milling machines would not get their start in England,

233
00:19:44,620 --> 00:19:47,160
but in the newly independent United States.

234
00:19:49,600 --> 00:19:51,046
In the 1800s, E.M.

235
00:19:51,047 --> 00:19:58,760
Remington and sons used their machine tools not only to make guns, but also to make typewriters.

236
00:19:59,960 --> 00:20:02,900
Machine tools will return on Modern Marvels.

237
00:20:05,780 --> 00:20:15,280
After the American Revolution, the British tried to maintain their industrial superiority by prohibiting machine tool exports to the United States.

238
00:20:15,281 --> 00:20:23,760
after the revolutionary war a lot of the technology was banned from the united states in the same way

239
00:20:23,761 --> 00:20:29,800
that we have embargoes against cuba or people would like embargoes against china today

240
00:20:29,801 --> 00:20:36,840
if the political situation had changed then the technology would have come in

241
00:20:36,841 --> 00:20:43,400
you know very freely we were the up and coming yankees england often looked down upon the united

242
00:20:43,401 --> 00:20:47,839
States in the types of machines that they produced, that they were inferior.

243
00:20:47,840 --> 00:20:48,540
They weren't

244
00:20:48,541 --> 00:20:54,160
of the quality of the British machines. And in some respects, that's very true.

245
00:20:54,161 --> 00:20:54,861
We didn't

246
00:20:54,862 --> 00:21:00,320
produce the types of these large, sturdy machine tools that the British did, but in many respects

247
00:21:00,321 --> 00:21:04,940
that worked in our favor because the machine tool industry changed so rapidly in the 19th

248
00:21:04,941 --> 00:21:08,869
century that we could afford to build little cheaper machines.

249
00:21:08,870 --> 00:21:10,580
Within 10 to 20 years, they

250
00:21:10,581 --> 00:21:13,815
were on to something new and different, and those machines were replaced.

251
00:21:13,816 --> 00:21:14,516
Whereas I think

252
00:21:14,517 --> 00:21:18,860
many instances, the British stayed with the world reliable machines, and we surpassed

253
00:21:18,861 --> 00:21:19,561
them.

254
00:21:23,620 --> 00:21:28,020
Credit for inventing the milling machine is usually given to the famous American inventor

255
00:21:28,021 --> 00:21:32,780
Eli Whitney. But it's credit, some say, he doesn't deserve.

256
00:21:32,781 --> 00:21:35,120
Whitney was born in Westboro,

257
00:21:35,121 --> 00:21:42,040
Massachusetts in 1765 and became famous for his new cotton gin, used to remove the seeds

258
00:21:42,041 --> 00:21:43,896
from raw cotton plants.

259
00:21:43,897 --> 00:21:48,900
While Whitney held the cotton gin patent and the machines were

260
00:21:48,901 --> 00:21:54,100
legally his alone to make, the ease with which they could be copied made them the target

261
00:21:54,101 --> 00:21:54,907
of pirates.

262
00:21:54,908 --> 00:22:00,780
To cover his legal bills defending the cotton gin, Whitney started manufacturing

263
00:22:00,781 --> 00:22:01,481
rifles.

264
00:22:01,482 --> 00:22:07,480
He built the first milling machine to help fulfill a government contract calling

265
00:22:07,481 --> 00:22:14,299
for him to manufacture 10,000 muskets in a record-breaking two years.

266
00:22:14,300 --> 00:22:15,880
But some say the

267
00:22:15,881 --> 00:22:21,560
great Eli Whitney borrowed his design from another American gun manufacturer, John Hall.

268
00:22:22,220 --> 00:22:26,800
Government contracts said any machine tools used in gun manufacture could be copied by

269
00:22:26,801 --> 00:22:31,000
others, so long as they were used to help make more guns.

270
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:38,060
Many believe Hall had the first milling machine and that Whitney simply borrowed his design.

271
00:22:38,840 --> 00:22:42,520
But being more famous, Whitney ended up with the credit.

272
00:22:43,980 --> 00:22:49,520
I don't think they worried too much about whether somebody stole a patent or not, simply

273
00:22:49,521 --> 00:22:52,460
because communications aren't what they are today.

274
00:22:52,461 --> 00:23:01,460
I think that there is a tremendous amount of adaptation from one builder to another.

275
00:23:05,460 --> 00:23:09,380
In many ways, milling machines do the work of a planer or shaper.

276
00:23:10,240 --> 00:23:12,240
Metal is cut smooth by a spinning blade.

277
00:23:13,100 --> 00:23:19,300
But unlike a planer or shaper, a milling machine does not have to back up after each cut, and that makes it faster.

278
00:23:22,460 --> 00:23:25,720
The milling machine is pretty much the brains of the machine tool shop.

279
00:23:26,020 --> 00:23:28,800
It's used for making precise cuts.

280
00:23:29,620 --> 00:23:33,580
This one is a horizontal mill or what's sometimes referred to as a slab mill.

281
00:23:34,520 --> 00:23:38,400
You can adjust the stock that you're working on on any axis.

282
00:23:39,440 --> 00:23:44,960
This table is moving the stock against the rotating sharpened steel tool.

283
00:23:44,961 --> 00:23:51,500
The steel tool basically chips away or scrapes away small particles of the metal.

284
00:23:52,460 --> 00:23:58,940
Any slab of metal that needed to be milled off to a uniform height would be milled on a horizontal mill like this.

285
00:24:02,440 --> 00:24:07,380
The Cincinnati Screw and Tap Company built its first milling machine in 1878.

286
00:24:08,380 --> 00:24:13,160
The company would soon become the largest manufacturer of milling machines in the world.

287
00:24:13,760 --> 00:24:21,140
A really big break for the company came just about the turn of the century with the advent of manufacturers of bicycles.

288
00:24:21,141 --> 00:24:24,140
and the bicycle parts, of course, are made of metal

289
00:24:24,141 --> 00:24:27,820
and there was a need in the growing bicycle industry

290
00:24:27,821 --> 00:24:31,040
to have milling machines to make the components for bicycles.

291
00:24:34,200 --> 00:24:38,100
Milling machines also became a key part of the early automotive industry

292
00:24:38,101 --> 00:24:42,700
when Henry Ford used them in his factory to help manufacture the first Model Ts.

293
00:24:45,900 --> 00:24:49,420
Because the milling machine was more efficient than planers and shapers,

294
00:24:50,280 --> 00:24:54,260
By the mid-1900s, it had replaced them in most machine shops.

295
00:24:56,540 --> 00:25:00,600
World War I and World War II propelled milling machine manufacturers

296
00:25:00,601 --> 00:25:03,720
to make unprecedented numbers of these valuable machines.

297
00:25:05,340 --> 00:25:09,500
The production of airplanes, or the production of jeeps,

298
00:25:09,501 --> 00:25:13,740
or the production of personnel carriers, or the production of tanks,

299
00:25:14,340 --> 00:25:19,100
or even the production of guns and arms, is all dependent on metal parts.

300
00:25:19,420 --> 00:25:21,000
mass-produced metal parts.

301
00:25:21,840 --> 00:25:26,440
In World War I, Cincinnati was very busy making basic machine tools,

302
00:25:26,920 --> 00:25:30,460
and we were making them for the factories that made or goods.

303
00:25:31,140 --> 00:25:34,940
In World War II, we were also extremely busy here

304
00:25:34,941 --> 00:25:40,160
in making those same machine tools to support not just the U.S.,

305
00:25:40,161 --> 00:25:45,020
but we were also supporting the Allies with machine tools during that period of time.

306
00:25:45,021 --> 00:25:53,320
And we were actually making components, portions of tanks, portions of personnel carriers.

307
00:25:53,460 --> 00:25:57,660
We were actually making those components in the factory at the same time we were making machine tools.

308
00:26:01,100 --> 00:26:05,620
After the war, milling machines were retooled to make consumer goods,

309
00:26:06,040 --> 00:26:10,120
one of the reasons for the explosion in the standard of living in the 1950s.

310
00:26:10,121 --> 00:26:18,600
Within decades, milling machines had reached heights of precision and power never dreamed of before.

311
00:26:19,980 --> 00:26:23,620
Modern milling machines evolved into complete machining centers

312
00:26:23,621 --> 00:26:27,280
that could perform multiple functions on a part as it was milled.

313
00:26:27,960 --> 00:26:34,500
The precision with which you can make metal parts that fit together in a tight fashion

314
00:26:34,501 --> 00:26:40,140
is solely dependent on the precision accuracy capabilities

315
00:26:40,141 --> 00:26:42,960
of the machine tool that you use to make those parts.

316
00:26:43,960 --> 00:26:46,540
The more accurate the machine tool is,

317
00:26:46,960 --> 00:26:50,760
the more precisely you can control the dimensions of parts

318
00:26:50,761 --> 00:26:55,180
that ultimately mate together in an engine or in an aircraft

319
00:26:55,181 --> 00:26:56,940
or whatever the case may be.

320
00:26:56,941 --> 00:27:07,400
Precise, interchangeable parts are a concept we now take for granted.

321
00:27:08,860 --> 00:27:14,660
But prior to Whitney's milling machine, supposedly identical parts would always differ slightly.

322
00:27:15,480 --> 00:27:17,940
A machinist would have to shave the part for a match.

323
00:27:20,020 --> 00:27:26,060
Whitney claimed his milling machine was so precise that any part from any musket could be interchanged with any other.

324
00:27:26,940 --> 00:27:29,680
He called the concept interchangeable parts.

325
00:27:31,540 --> 00:27:35,420
Thomas Jefferson accepted Whitney's claims after examining ten muskets,

326
00:27:36,020 --> 00:27:39,660
although some say Whitney overstated the interchangeability of his parts.

327
00:27:41,340 --> 00:27:42,440
They were terrible.

328
00:27:43,200 --> 00:27:47,260
Jefferson was an enthusiastic amateur, but there's a lot of things he didn't know.

329
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:51,120
And one thing he didn't know about was interchangeability.

330
00:27:52,100 --> 00:27:56,500
Whitney made one gun and ten locks that supposedly would fit it.

331
00:27:56,940 --> 00:28:01,560
Well, that's easy enough to do if you've spent time enough and money enough to do it.

332
00:28:01,600 --> 00:28:03,100
And he saved the contract that way.

333
00:28:03,500 --> 00:28:05,780
He fooled Jefferson and the water department.

334
00:28:09,080 --> 00:28:13,140
Nevertheless, the concept of interchangeable parts was here to stay.

335
00:28:13,660 --> 00:28:20,020
Soon, many gun manufacturers, including Robbins & Lawrence, Springfield, and Colt, had achieved it.

336
00:28:22,820 --> 00:28:26,820
While the milling machine brought about a new precision in the products it created,

337
00:28:26,940 --> 00:28:29,320
It didn't accomplish this feat alone.

338
00:28:30,220 --> 00:28:33,560
There were four other machine tools that played a critical role.

339
00:28:36,560 --> 00:28:42,780
Eli Whitney's milling machine was so well designed, it did not change for 150 years.

340
00:28:44,180 --> 00:28:47,000
Machine tools will return on Modern Marvels.

341
00:28:49,520 --> 00:28:51,860
Drilling is a very old art.

342
00:28:51,861 --> 00:28:57,400
The crudest form of drilling is the bow, a method familiar to most Boy Scouts.

343
00:28:59,620 --> 00:29:03,360
One of the earliest improvements in drilling was a bit with a twisted groove,

344
00:29:04,120 --> 00:29:06,020
so shavings would rise from the hole.

345
00:29:09,280 --> 00:29:12,980
The blacksmith's post drill was one of the earliest modern drills.

346
00:29:14,240 --> 00:29:19,640
This is a post drill, and as with any tool, it eliminates a lot of work from your task.

347
00:29:19,641 --> 00:29:24,040
instead of having to push down on a drill that you might have had to push down with your shoulder

348
00:29:24,041 --> 00:29:25,820
and get maybe an inaccurate hole.

349
00:29:26,180 --> 00:29:29,900
The post drill mounts firmly and securely on a post in a shop,

350
00:29:30,360 --> 00:29:34,320
and then the power is kept constant when you're turning it by the flywheel.

351
00:29:36,800 --> 00:29:41,180
It was a simple step to connect the drill's flywheel to a steam-powered belt.

352
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:44,320
Soon, power drills were in every machine shop.

353
00:29:44,900 --> 00:29:46,860
They became the fourth type of machine tool.

354
00:29:47,680 --> 00:29:51,080
For drills, advancement came not so much in the improvement of the machine,

355
00:29:51,680 --> 00:29:53,520
but in the metal that made the cutting bit.

356
00:29:55,180 --> 00:30:00,180
The early steel, made of iron and carbon, was hard, but it could be brittle.

357
00:30:02,020 --> 00:30:05,000
After crucible steel was invented in 1751,

358
00:30:05,600 --> 00:30:08,560
in which alloy steels could be created in a sealed furnace,

359
00:30:08,920 --> 00:30:14,440
drill bits could be made harder and were able to drill holes in harder metal stock.

360
00:30:14,441 --> 00:30:23,520
By the early 1900s, drilling machines were being used to build sewing machines, bicycles, and watches.

361
00:30:25,220 --> 00:30:29,980
Drills that could cut 30 or more holes at a time were used to machine engine blocks.

362
00:30:32,260 --> 00:30:36,000
Hole making happens to be the most common metalworking operation in industry.

363
00:30:36,540 --> 00:30:37,440
We make a lot of holes.

364
00:30:38,320 --> 00:30:42,920
Drilling machines come in all kinds of sizes because we make small holes, we make very large holes.

365
00:30:42,921 --> 00:30:49,920
Hole making is just so common that it's essential to manufacturing in almost every industry.

366
00:30:49,921 --> 00:30:56,920
I think some of the most impressive drilling machines today are those designed for high-speed hole making.

367
00:30:56,921 --> 00:31:02,920
Typically these drill at speeds that almost, you can hardly see the hole making process at so fast.

368
00:31:02,921 --> 00:31:06,920
Certain work pieces require hundreds, even thousands of holes.

369
00:31:06,921 --> 00:31:11,920
So the faster those holes can be made, the more efficient the drilling machine has to be.

370
00:31:12,920 --> 00:31:17,560
The advances of the 1800s that led to better drills

371
00:31:17,561 --> 00:31:21,360
also led to a new type of machine tool, the grinding machine.

372
00:31:22,540 --> 00:31:28,080
As early as the 1500s, Leonardo da Vinci was drawing sketches of primitive grinders.

373
00:31:29,020 --> 00:31:33,280
For hundreds of years, man has used forms of them to sharpen his tools.

374
00:31:36,720 --> 00:31:41,920
In 1837, James Naismith, the same Naismith who invented the shaper,

375
00:31:41,921 --> 00:31:44,360
patented the first machine grinder.

376
00:31:46,400 --> 00:31:49,580
Charles Norton was one of the most famous manufacturers of grinders.

377
00:31:50,240 --> 00:31:52,040
When his employers, Brown and Sharp,

378
00:31:52,420 --> 00:31:55,100
were not interested in developing a large production grinder,

379
00:31:55,620 --> 00:31:56,980
Norton set out on his own.

380
00:31:58,460 --> 00:32:01,720
In 1903, he developed the Norton grinding machine.

381
00:32:03,000 --> 00:32:07,200
It could grind out an automobile crankshaft in just 15 minutes,

382
00:32:07,201 --> 00:32:09,800
a job that previously took five hours.

383
00:32:10,760 --> 00:32:12,480
It was a tremendous success.

384
00:32:13,180 --> 00:32:15,860
Henry Ford ordered 35 for his Model T plant.

385
00:32:19,400 --> 00:32:22,540
Grinders are best suited to work on extremely hard metal.

386
00:32:23,760 --> 00:32:28,980
Once steel is machined, it is often heated and cooled in a process that can cause distortions.

387
00:32:30,260 --> 00:32:34,120
Grinders are one of the few tools that can machine this heat-treated steel.

388
00:32:34,121 --> 00:32:41,560
Grinding is generally a process that's applied when extreme accuracy is very important

389
00:32:41,561 --> 00:32:47,360
because grinding can be highly controlled and it can be used to achieve levels of precision

390
00:32:47,361 --> 00:32:49,800
that would be impossible with other types of machine tools.

391
00:32:53,980 --> 00:32:58,720
Today, grinders are used by the aerospace industry to shape metals like titanium,

392
00:32:58,721 --> 00:33:05,720
Inconel and Hastelloy, extremely hard metals designed to resist high temperatures.

393
00:33:05,721 --> 00:33:09,720
These modern grinders are the most accurate of all machine tools.

394
00:33:09,721 --> 00:33:16,720
The best can grind a part to within one ten-thousandth of an inch.

395
00:33:16,721 --> 00:33:22,720
The sixth type of machine tool is the most simple, the power saw.

396
00:33:22,721 --> 00:33:27,720
The first steam-powered sawmill was built in London in 1663.

397
00:33:27,721 --> 00:33:30,380
It was built not to cut metal, but wood.

398
00:33:31,320 --> 00:33:35,900
It caused riots among woodcutters, who thought the power saw would replace them.

399
00:33:37,160 --> 00:33:41,980
Not to worry, it took another century for steam-powered sawmills to reach widespread use.

400
00:33:43,460 --> 00:33:46,780
By the mid-1700s, as blades became harder,

401
00:33:47,660 --> 00:33:51,660
power saws were installed in most factories to saw metal before it was machined.

402
00:33:51,661 --> 00:34:01,920
In the early 19th century, the invention of a power bandsaw was made possible by the improved flexibility of steel.

403
00:34:03,400 --> 00:34:09,720
The saw's rotating blade was flexible enough to wrap around rotary wheels, yet hard enough to stay sharp.

404
00:34:11,720 --> 00:34:16,540
Power saws are often the first machine tool that raw material is going to counter,

405
00:34:16,760 --> 00:34:21,520
even though they may not have the same glamour that other types of machines have.

406
00:34:21,660 --> 00:34:25,460
They're still extremely important parts of the manufacturing process

407
00:34:25,461 --> 00:34:29,080
because they essentially create the work pieces

408
00:34:29,081 --> 00:34:33,860
that will go to other types of machine tools for complete manufacturing.

409
00:34:35,800 --> 00:34:39,500
The seventh and final category of machine tool, the punch press,

410
00:34:39,660 --> 00:34:41,540
wasn't designed to cut or shave metal.

411
00:34:42,000 --> 00:34:43,580
It was made to press it into shape.

412
00:34:45,420 --> 00:34:48,680
Metalworking is broken down into two different types.

413
00:34:48,681 --> 00:34:57,540
one you have the production of parts by the removal of metal in the form of chips

414
00:34:57,541 --> 00:35:04,160
and that's what we do on a lathe on a milling machine on a planer but then beyond that you

415
00:35:04,161 --> 00:35:13,700
have a form of metal working which is the forming of metal by pressure so that you use a press then

416
00:35:13,701 --> 00:35:16,280
to form it against a die.

417
00:35:20,300 --> 00:35:23,680
Punch presses were one of the final machine tools to be developed.

418
00:35:25,020 --> 00:35:27,380
They have heavy rams on a flywheel

419
00:35:27,381 --> 00:35:30,100
that when triggered, press down against a base.

420
00:35:31,800 --> 00:35:33,780
They shape metal over a die,

421
00:35:34,160 --> 00:35:37,800
a hard metal block that forms the metal into a particular shape.

422
00:35:39,180 --> 00:35:40,540
When I was young,

423
00:35:40,541 --> 00:35:45,800
my father wouldn't allow me to leave the car door open.

424
00:35:46,420 --> 00:35:49,420
I'd open the car for some reason and leave it hanging on the hinges.

425
00:35:50,220 --> 00:35:50,940
Nothing doing.

426
00:35:51,860 --> 00:35:52,840
That was a wooden body.

427
00:35:55,220 --> 00:35:57,940
And it didn't hold its shape, so you had to slam the door.

428
00:35:58,660 --> 00:36:03,140
But nowadays, you can let the door hang on to it all through your body.

429
00:36:03,940 --> 00:36:07,480
And as the volume of cars went up, they started making the parts and presses.

430
00:36:07,481 --> 00:36:11,360
instead of woodworking shops.

431
00:36:13,720 --> 00:36:18,220
Again, machine tools played a major role in World War I and World War II

432
00:36:18,221 --> 00:36:22,580
as punch presses formed parts for tanks, jeeps, ammunition casings,

433
00:36:22,760 --> 00:36:24,320
and countless other war products.

434
00:36:29,300 --> 00:36:33,040
Today, as punch presses have become more complex and precise,

435
00:36:33,041 --> 00:36:38,880
They are used to shape metal into every conceivable shape for every conceivable use.

436
00:36:40,500 --> 00:36:44,020
Next, the new ultra-modern machine tools.

437
00:36:46,120 --> 00:36:52,480
During World War II, the machine tool industry built 800,000 new machine tools.

438
00:36:53,620 --> 00:36:56,620
Machine tools will return on Modern Marvels.

439
00:36:57,880 --> 00:37:01,700
While machine tools remain as a legacy of the Industrial Revolution,

440
00:37:02,300 --> 00:37:08,020
Since the 1960s, they have taken on a new level of sophistication, rivaling their initial design.

441
00:37:09,340 --> 00:37:12,340
The most dramatic changes have to do with how they are controlled.

442
00:37:15,820 --> 00:37:22,160
The earliest machine tools used flywheels to control the position of the tool and the speed at which it cut.

443
00:37:24,520 --> 00:37:30,860
Beginning in the early 1950s, those flywheels came under the control of paper tapes and punch cards.

444
00:37:30,861 --> 00:37:33,980
used to trigger a precise numeric routine.

445
00:37:35,240 --> 00:37:37,540
That routine could be easily repeated.

446
00:37:38,680 --> 00:37:40,700
The process was called numeric control.

447
00:37:42,840 --> 00:37:45,500
Numeric control was invented in 1947

448
00:37:45,501 --> 00:37:50,540
when John Parsons put a milling machine under the control of an IBM punch card.

449
00:37:51,240 --> 00:37:53,880
It spawned a whole new world of machine tools.

450
00:37:55,200 --> 00:37:59,120
The biggest change in machine tools occurred with the introduction of numerical control.

451
00:37:59,840 --> 00:38:04,320
That basically made it possible to automate the entire machining operation.

452
00:38:05,060 --> 00:38:08,840
Instead of relying on the operator turning hand wheels,

453
00:38:08,841 --> 00:38:15,660
the motion of the machine is basically directed by the numbers that are read off the tape

454
00:38:15,661 --> 00:38:22,660
and then translated into corresponding motions that will duplicate the shape that the designer had in mind.

455
